  "worked_solution": "The center of the expansion is $x=-2,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $1,$ i.e., $R\\ge 1.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-3,-1].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-3,-1].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041629/assets/93ab98aaa7170e5fcef1c090555f259b2030384b6f2f25a282af9d8b6a406ed2.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $-\\frac{3}{2}\\in (-3,-1],$ the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-3\\notin (-3,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $-\\frac{7}{2}\\notin (-3,-1],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
